BRepBlend_Walking: a walk that ends on both faces' restrictions at once
takes the end parameter of a closed guide only where the guide's end lies
near the section found, and a walk that ends on one restriction corrects
its end only to an end of that restriction near the point found on it
(mitcad#2272, GitHub mitcad/Mitcad#582).

The case: a nozzle of radius 15 on a dome that runs on tangentially into a
tube of the same radius (50), the nozzle's axis on the circle where they
meet, its foot rounded with 1.5. The foot is a closed tangent chain of two
edges, one on the dome, one on the tube, whose two vertices lie on that
circle; the nozzle's side is one face closed round its axis, its seam
through one of the two vertices. The rounding's walk on the faces of the
chain's first edge (the dome or the tube and the nozzle's side) ends at the
chain's other vertex, where its contact on the body reaches the circle (the
face's restriction) and, where the seam runs through that vertex, its
contact on the nozzle the seam (the other face's restriction): both at
once (Blend_OnRst12).

There, for a periodic guide, the walk tests the guide's ends saved before
its extension (ChFiDS_ElSpine::GetSavedFirstParameter and
GetSavedLastParameter) by the plane through the guide's point at the end
and the section's two points: where that plane is normal to the guide at
the end, the end's parameter is taken. Both vertices of the foot and the
section's points lie in the plane of the circle, normal to the guide at
both vertices, so the section at the chain's other vertex, half a turn from
the guide's ends, was given the parameter 0 or the period (the trace: the
walk's line from w 0 to 0, or to the period, with 30 points along half the
foot) and the stripe failed (Failure_Stripe). The rounding built only when
the chain's first edge started at the seam's vertex, where the parameter
taken is the right one: picked by one of its edges on three of the four
nozzles, by neither on the fourth.

The guide's end is now taken only where the guide's point at the end lies
within the distance between the section's two points (at least 10 times
the walk's 3d tolerance) of the guide's point at the parameter found.

The same plane tricked the end on one restriction. Where the nozzle lies
across the dome's and the tube's seams (its foot then has four edges) and
the chain's first edge starts at the vertex the nozzle's seam does not run
through, the walk's first piece, going back from its start, ends at that
vertex on the circle (the body's restriction) alone.
CorrectExtremityOnOneRst (OCC25701) then takes the end of the restriction
nearest the guide: the restriction is the circle between the foot's two
vertices, both on the guide, and the first in the order of its ends was
the other vertex; its plane test passed (the same plane), and the piece
started at the parameter of the vertex half a turn away (w -43.9 instead
of 0). The rounding failed picked by that edge.

Now an end of the restriction is taken only where it lies within twice the
distance between the point found on the restriction and the guide's point
at the parameter found (at least 10 times the walk's 3d tolerance) of that
point; without such an end nothing is corrected.

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2272.
